{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1538076224,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNTIxMzMyNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2880#issuecomment-425213324"},"message":"Quick Q: is it just many subdatasets, or are they also deeply nested?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"3d4093ba1852d0599c7b70904b94d21d5cf02d05"},"timestamp":1538078521,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNTIyNDEzNg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2880#issuecomment-425224136"},"message":"Also nested, though not that deeply (2-3 levels?)   Basically I'm trying to consolidate all my projects in one place.\n\nSo, I understand that if I have datasets A, A/B, A/B/C, A/D, A/D/E, then C has to be saved before B and E before D; but the A/B/* and A/D/* branches can be done in parallel.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1538079837,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNTIzMDM1Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2880#issuecomment-425230353"},"message":"yeah. there is a quite a number of places (save, install, remove, drop, ...) where we could parallelize and greatly improve performance and UX.  Since majority of work is to be done underneath by git/git-annex, even \"simple\" threading could probably be sufficient to provide palatalization. None of the code though is written with thread safety in mind AFAIK, so there could be surprises ;-)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"3d4093ba1852d0599c7b70904b94d21d5cf02d05"},"timestamp":1538317844,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNTcyNDc2NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2880#issuecomment-425724764"},"message":"\" None of the code though is written with thread safety in mind AFAIK\" -- at least for read-only operations like diff, this shouldn't be a concern?\ngit-annex itself is said to be thread-safe; on the other hand, this isn't currently tested ( https://git-annex.branchable.com/git-annex-test/#comment-7315b575dc4c77ac5abf7186423584ec )...\nI tend to work on machines with at least 8 cores, often more, so parallelizing would certainly help.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1538318183,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNTcyNTE0MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2880#issuecomment-425725141"},"message":"This is already on my list. ATM I am working one level deeper, once this is done, I'll come up for this (ping #2428)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"3d4093ba1852d0599c7b70904b94d21d5cf02d05"},"timestamp":1538318238,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNTcyNTIxMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2880#issuecomment-425725213"},"message":"@mih thanks a lot!","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1538476979,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNjIyODY2NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2880#issuecomment-426228665"},"message":"Q on parallelization within datalad vs in git-annex:\n\nMany relevant annex command can take a `--jobs` argument. In many cases we do not expose this in datalad ATM. If we are adding parallelization in datalad no, we have to think about avoiding oversubscription of resources.\n\nA standard paradigm would be to use a pool of \"workers\", where the number of workers is limited by hardware specs and/or config. However, if we manage this pool in datalad, we would likely have no other choice but to limit git-annex to `--jobs 1`.\n\nI wonder what is more advantageous: keep datalad single-threaded and (more opportunistically) use git-annexes features, or vice versa? Leaving it to git-annex certainly seems simpler. Any intuitions on how these two might compare re speed-up potential?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1538558910,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNjU3MDM1Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2880#issuecomment-426570353"},"message":"Not really sure either. My intuition is in favor of using annex' parallelization as much as possible. Simply because it's done at a low-level. Let's say we have to add files in a number of datasets. It still seems unlikely to me, that the sheer number of datasets is what is limiting speed, but the number of files involved. If that number actually is low, I wouldn't expect parallelization to matter at all. Annex can do the parallelization on file level anyway, speeding up the time per dataset. The only thing annex can't do but we can is determining which datasets can be dealt with in parallel. The part we have to contribute here is certainly less critical. So, in order to be actually faster we would need to replicate annex' parallelization in addition. Being able to do both should perform best, but takes a lot of effort to figure what number of jobs to assign per call. The factor with higher impact is the one annex already provides though.","files":null}]}